sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 3, docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-default-instance): the automatic development deployment of docs/reference/deployments.md, in the default Instance's container. Swamp's policy finds the commit when its shared producer is active; the Gateway admits the deployment and records its result. When the charts move into place, this becomes the development branch of the deploy chart. The default of a nested-fleet Project deploys after each Gateway release instead, with its inner fleet: container/fleet-refresh.mmd.
  %% Delivered state, 2026-11-11: the independent development tick (#3331) was reverted. Development auto-deploy stays in the shared automatic-deploy producer and depends on its policy setting. A Gateway release does not deploy development defaults. A clone starts at the last successfully deployed seed; instance:deploy on the container default explicitly refreshes it. The independent container-default policy remains the target.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · automatic deployment policy
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>policy · execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  Note over SW: Policy tick finds a commit<br/>native schedule
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Swamp's automatic deployment policy reads the default branch head and the checks, as it does today; it owns discovery, backoff and the retry limit
  SW->>GW: Admit the deployment<br/>instance:deploy-automatic
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Gateway admits `instance.deploy` for that exact commit, with the Instance lock and a repository read token in the vault
  Note over GW: Capture source facts<br/>before the write lock
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads before the write transaction; admission rechecks eligibility, source configuration and idempotency before recording facts; see /reference/deployments#container-source-facts
  %%> - refuses admission when capture or source classification fails
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  opt the default Instance is asleep
    SW->>N: Start the container<br/>incus start
  end
  %%> - the start waits only for the units the container's boot starts: FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units; a new or newly running Process is the next step's work
  opt nested-fleet container
    SW->>C: Deliver the Club key<br/>Ansible · tags start
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - pipelined module stdin into the container's /run (a tmpfs, never a snapshot), before any check of the inner fleet
    %%> - the result's `nested_fleet_club_key` is what this write did (`written`, `written_serve_not_restarted`, `unchanged`, `refused`); without a vault copy it says `missing`
  end
  SW->>N: Fetch into the mirror<br/>Ansible · tags fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fetches into the Node's mirror and pushes the branch into the container as a bundle; the token never enters the container
  %%> - a pinned commit off the branch's first-parent chain refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same checks as a checkout default: a dirty tree refuses `deployment.checkout_dirty`, a diverged branch `deployment.branch_diverged`
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Project's development deploy steps, as `orbit`: they install changed dependencies and migrate
  SW->>C: Install the Processes<br/>Ansible · tags processes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - installs the Project's new and changed development Process definitions as `orbit-*` units, with their environment files from the vault
  %%> - restarts a changed unit and removes a former environment file
  %%> - removes the unit and environment folder of a Process whose definition the Project removed; admission marked its record `removing`
  %%> - enables and starts each running unit and waits for it; a unit that does not become active fails with its name in the detail, and only its Process is recorded failed
  SW->>N: Take the seed snapshot<br/>Ansible · tags seed
  %%> - prepares a Node/Vite default’s private Node store as orbit before the existing snapshot stop/start boundary; this conversion itself never restarts a Process
  %%> - a converted store requires a new seed even at the same commit, so clones inherit it; older seeds remain slower but correct until the explicit deploy refreshes them
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops the default's Processes for a moment, snapshots the container and starts them again, so clones copy no half-written file
  %%> - every passed deployment retakes `seed-<commit>`, also at the same commit, so a clone copies the default's current caches and built files
  %%> - deletes older seed snapshots that no clone uses
  %%> - a default with an inner volume: the volume's snapshot of the same name is the other half of the seed; it is taken right after the container's, retaken when it is missing, and the older halves go only after both new halves exist. A failed volume snapshot deletes both new halves and fails with `instance.seed_volume_snapshot_failed`
  %%> - starting the units again restarts every running Process on the new commit, such as a queue worker
  SW->>C: Check the container<br/>Ansible · tags check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the branch is checked out at the commit and the units are active, else `deployment.branch_changed` or `deployment.processes_inactive`
  alt every required step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - deletes the Processes marked `removing` and names them in `processes_removed`
    %%> - new clones start at this snapshot; existing clones do not change
  else a required step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - no new seed snapshot is taken, so clones keep starting at the last commit that deployed; Swamp's policy retries after its backoff
  end
